 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Evaluation of SH coefficients is $O(1)$ per Gaussian per frame, as it involves a fixed number of polynomial evaluations [S1, unofficial]. The cost is negligible compared to rasterization [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Each Gaussian stores 48 floats (192 bytes) for SH coefficients at degree 3 [S1, S4, unofficial]. This is a significant memory overhead compared to view-independent RGB (12 bytes) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** On the Mip-NeRF360 benchmark, 3DGS with SH reaches 27.21 dB PSNR and 0.214 LPIPS at 134 fps [S1, unofficial]. Removing SH reduces PSNR to 25.35 on Average-30K [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Scaling:** SH evaluation scales linearly with the number of Gaussians and is highly parallelizable on GPUs [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Captures view-dependent effects like highlights and reflections efficiently [S1, unofficial][S2]. Closed-form evaluation allows for fast rendering [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** High memory bandwidth usage for degree 3 SH [S1, unofficial]. May overfit to high-frequency details if not regularized [S9, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When view-dependent appearance (specularities, reflections) is important for photorealism [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When memory bandwidth is a critical bottleneck and view-dependent effects are minimal [S1, unofficial].
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+- Memory: Each Gaussian stores 48 coefficients (16 per channel × 3 channels) [S1, unofficial]. This is significantly more than a single RGB triplet (3 floats) [S1, unofficial].
+- Time: Evaluation is a fixed number of polynomial operations per Gaussian, described as costing "almost nothing at display time" [S1, unofficial]. However, loading 48 floats per Gaussian can cause memory bandwidth bottlenecks [S1, unofficial].
+
+**Measured Performance:**
+- On the Average-30K dataset, using full SH (degree 3) achieves a PSNR of 26.05, whereas removing SH (No-SH) reduces PSNR to 25.35 [S6, unofficial].
+- On the Blender dataset, using SH yields a PSNR of 30.3 dB, SSIM of 0.945, and LPIPS of 0.078, compared to 27.1 dB, 0.932, and 0.155 without SH [S8].
+
+**Strengths and Limitations:**
+- Strengths: Captures view-dependent effects like highlights and reflections [S1, unofficial]. Compact representation compared to neural networks [S1, unofficial].
+- Limitations: High memory usage for high degrees [S1, unofficial]. Can introduce artifacts if not trained properly (e.g., weird colors on poorly observed sides) [S2, unofficial].
+
+**When to use:**
+- When view-dependent appearance (specularities, reflections) is important for photorealism [S1, unofficial].
+- When rendering speed is critical and neural network queries are too slow [S1, unofficial].
+
+**When not to use:**
+- When memory bandwidth is the primary bottleneck and view-dependent effects are negligible [S1, unofficial].
+- When only diffuse, view-independent color is required [S3].
 
 ## Background
-Spherical Harmonics (SH) are an orthogonal basis for functions on the unit sphere, repurposed from irradiance environment maps to represent the view-dependent color of 3D Gaussians [S1, S5, unofficial]. The method was introduced in the 3D Gaussian Splatting paper (Kerbl et al., 2023) [S1, unofficial]. It allows each Gaussian to store a color that varies with viewing direction, solving the problem of directional color variation compactly [S1, unofficial][S2]. The technique is distinct from classical view synthesis methods like light-field rendering, which require dense capture [S1, unofficial].
+Spherical harmonics were repurposed from irradiance environment maps to represent the view-dependent color of 3D Gaussians in the 3D Gaussian Splatting method [S1, unofficial]. The method was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S1, unofficial]. The SH expansion is analogous to a Fourier series on the sphere, where low-degree harmonics capture smooth diffuse shading and higher degrees capture sharper directional variations [S1, unofficial].
 
